From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pkgdemon <jpm820@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 03:05:09 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 85/85] NextBSD: g_part: allow GEOM::kerneldump on a UFS
 partition for the file dumper

g_part answers GEOM::kerneldump only on a partition whose scheme says it
can take dumps (G_PART_DUMPTO: swap and dump types) or when the request
comes from a nested scheme. The file-backed kernel dumper asks for the
dump routine under a UFS filesystem through the filesystem's own g_vfs
consumer, so on a partitioned disk it was refused with ENODEV.

Also allow the request on a freebsd-ufs partition when it was issued by
a g_vfs ("VFS" class) consumer, either directly or through pass-through
geoms such as labels, which clone the request (the root of the bio's
parent chain is checked). The dumper writes only the blocks of its
pinned file, so the rest of the filesystem is never touched. Every other
request keeps the swap-type check and its "not suitable for kernel
dumps" message.

UFS only. A ZFS dump path would need the same for freebsd-zfs and is
left for a follow-up.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sys/geom/part/g_part.c | 31 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sys/geom/part/g_part.c b/sys/geom/part/g_part.c
index 88e44b33..487adc22 100644
--- a/sys/geom/part/g_part.c
+++ b/sys/geom/part/g_part.c
@@ -2217,6 +2217,31 @@ g_part_spoiled(struct g_consumer *cp)
 	g_part_wither(cp->geom, ENXIO);
 }
 
+/*
+ * NextBSD: the file-backed kernel dumper (kern_dumpfile.c) asks a
+ * filesystem's own g_vfs consumer for GEOM::kerneldump, to find the disk
+ * dump routine under a pinned dump file.  It writes only that file's
+ * blocks, so allow the request on a UFS partition when it was issued by a
+ * filesystem consumer, whether directly or through pass-through geoms such
+ * as labels above the partition.  Only UFS for now; ZFS has no file
+ * dumper yet.
+ */
+static bool
+g_part_filedump_ok(struct bio *bp, struct g_part_table *table,
+    struct g_part_entry *entry)
+{
+	struct bio *root;
+	char buf[64];
+
+	for (root = bp; root->bio_parent != NULL; root = root->bio_parent)
+		;
+	if (root->bio_from == NULL ||
+	    strcmp(root->bio_from->geom->class->name, "VFS") != 0)
+		return (false);
+	return (strcmp(G_PART_TYPE(table, entry, buf, sizeof(buf)),
+	    g_part_alias_name(G_PART_ALIAS_FREEBSD_UFS)) == 0);
+}
+
 static void
 g_part_start(struct bio *bp)
 {
@@ -2298,11 +2323,13 @@ g_part_start(struct bio *bp)
 			 * Check that the partition is suitable for kernel
 			 * dumps. Typically only swap partitions should be
 			 * used. If the request comes from the nested scheme
-			 * we allow dumping there as well.
+			 * we allow dumping there as well, and on a UFS
+			 * partition for the NextBSD file dumper.
 			 */
 			if ((bp->bio_from == NULL ||
 			    bp->bio_from->geom->class != &g_part_class) &&
-			    G_PART_DUMPTO(table, entry) == 0) {
+			    G_PART_DUMPTO(table, entry) == 0 &&
+			    !g_part_filedump_ok(bp, table, entry)) {
 				g_io_deliver(bp, ENODEV);
 				printf("GEOM_PART: Partition '%s' not suitable"
 				    " for kernel dumps (wrong type?)\n",
-- 
